-- 2025100101 (follow-up 1816): who may act on whom is asked here too.
--   _pental_manage_refusal says whether the caller may act on a person (below
--   them, or another administrator when they are the account owner; never
--   themselves, never the owner). rpc_admin_reset_mfa (both forms) asks it;
--   rpc_admin_reset_all_portal_mfa is the owner's alone; and
--   tr_pental_guard_profile_rank holds a session's direct writes on profiles
--   to the same rule for role, active, the sign-in binding and the address
--   (a senior could make themselves an administrator with one request).
-- 2025093003 (follow-up 1812): a passkey is checked in this database.
--   _webauthn_verify_assertion (ECDSA P-256 over SHA-256, the COSE key and
--   the DER signature parsed here, every structural check the gateway makes)
--   replaces the call to the WebAuthn verifier Pental hosted; the URL row,
--   its seeds and rpc_set_webauthn_verify_url are gone. rpc_apply_signed_settings
--   and rpc_apply_signed_bulk_clean call it; nothing is fetched from anywhere.
-- 2025093002 (follow-up 1810): Pental cannot reset or wipe a firm's portal
--   MFA. rpc_wipe_byo_webauthn is dropped; rpc_admin_reset_mfa(text, ...) and
--   rpc_clear_mfa accept a signed-in session with fresh MFA and nothing else;
--   rpc_apply_signed_settings no longer takes 'rpc_admin_reset_mfa' or
--   'rpc_clear_mfa' (and their two helpers are dropped); rpc_remove_webauthn
--   needs fresh MFA; the authenticator columns on profiles change only through
--   the MFA functions (tr_guard_mfa_columns); public.owner_reset_mfa is the
--   console-only recovery for a firm with no admin able to sign in.
-- 2025093001 (follow-up 1809): every MFA reset or clear routine needs a
--   factor presented this session.
-- 2025092909 (follow-up 1807): no transaction control of its own. The
--   hardening block's BEGIN and COMMIT are gone, so the whole file is one
--   transaction however it is applied, and a statement that fails leaves
--   nothing of the run behind (the version stays where it was). The
--   self-hosted kit's updater refuses a bundle that has any.
-- 2025092908 (follow-up 1806, after a second review): a passkey check and
--   its audit row are live during a pause; a write from a session with no API
--   request behind it marks an open run's copy as behind; files changed since
--   the pause began make the copy behind too (and pental.io sees it); the
--   status carries this database's own tag and the run it was closed for; the
--   pause's triggers are left alone when already right and wait at most ten
--   seconds for a lock; finish takes one foreign key and nothing after it.
-- 2025092907 (follow-up 1806, after independent review): the pause on the
--   source lets signing in carry on (sign-in bookkeeping tables and columns
--   are live; everything is refused once moved); a copy that fell behind (a
--   save past a lapsed pause) is refused at the last check before a switch,
--   and rpc_transfer_status carries the run tags pental.io matches; a
--   database moved away from is refused as a source unless recovering, and
--   stays closed if that run is given back; the move's record is revoked
--   from the service role and finish reads only table names and foreign keys
--   from it; json and jsonb travel as text (a JSON null stays one); the file
--   rows are paused where the storage service lets their owner.
-- 2025092906 (follow-up 1806): the move, reworked after review. A probe
--   refuses two addresses that reach one database; the source's API writes
--   are paused for the run (and refused for good once the portal has
--   switched away); a run id under a row lock; rows paged by a byte budget
--   and passed as text so no number is rounded; only the columns the rows
--   carry are written; user rows made right for GoTrue; file rows merged onto
--   the files the storage API wrote, never created bare; the finish taken one
--   step per call. The version stamp can no longer be written by a session.
-- 2025092905 (follow-up 1805): moving the database between homes. The
--   rpc_transfer_* functions (manifest, export, export_vault, begin, import,
--   import_vault, finish) let the firm's own browser copy every table, the
--   vault and the file metadata from one database to another with the
--   database's own service credential, which Pental never holds; nothing
--   answers a publishable key, a user session or the provisioning secret.
-- 2025092904 (follow-up 1803): the Pental gateway. This file now runs on a
--   plain Postgres (Azure Database for PostgreSQL, Cloud SQL, RDS, a server of
--   the firm's) behind the gateway: uuid-ossp and the Supabase Vault are used
--   where they exist and the gateway's own vault otherwise; _verify_pental_auth
--   and rpc_apply_signed_settings accept the gateway's attestation header
--   (_pental_attested, an HMAC under the pental_gateway_attest vault secret)
--   in place of the http verifier calls, which a managed Postgres cannot make.
--   On Supabase nothing changes: there is no such secret, and the header is
--   ignored.
-- 2025092903 (follow-up 1801): assessments.qa_reviewed (which findings the
--   reviewer has marked as reviewed, with the finding's row_version at the
--   time) and rpc_qa_mark_reviewed, which marks or unmarks a set of findings in
--   one statement as the caller.
-- 2025080500 (follow-up 712): the evidence rule moved into
--   `_pental_scan_in_blocks` and is now applied on ALL FOUR import write paths.
--   690/699/701/706 all landed on the INSERT and the title-key arm; the
--   issue-fingerprint append arm and the refresh branch never wrote `evidence`
--   at all, so an existing finding could not gain any however many times it was
--   rescanned. Also `rpc_scan_import` gained `p_full_report`: a scanner that
--   only reports what CHANGED (Burp Professional over the REST API, which runs
--   every scan into one shared project site map) must not have the closing pass
--   read its silence as a remediation.
-- 2025080499 (follow-up 706): the "host already listed" early return dropped
--   the evidence blocks. On a RESCAN — which is every finding that already
--   exists — the request and response were discarded exactly as before, which
--   is why three upstream fixes changed nothing.
-- 2025080498 (follow-up 701): _pental_evidence_host_row keeps the incoming
--   blocks instead of only flattening them into a table cell. It collapsed all
--   whitespace for a one-line cell, which destroys an HTTP exchange — and
--   returned only the table, so the proof never reached the finding.
-- 2025080497 (follow-up 699): scan evidence arriving as TEXT is wrapped into
--   the block shape the product renders. `_pental_evidence_host_row` takes
--   incoming BLOCKS and every adapter sends a STRING, so the argument was
--   always empty — the adapters extracted evidence and the import dropped it.
-- 2025080496 (follow-up 698): the KB reuse also takes CVSS, impact and
--   category. A scanner's CVSS is generic (Burp reported 0.0 for everything);
--   the entry's is the firm's considered view. Only when the entry HAS a score,
--   so a KB entry left at 0 cannot erase one a scanner calculated.
-- 2025080495 (follow-up 696): findings.scan_run_ids — the SET of runs that
--   reported a finding. 686 kept the first run's id so a new run stopped
--   listing recurring findings entirely; a finding is seen by many runs, so
--   every run that saw it lists it and none lose it when another does.
-- 2025080494 (follow-up 688): a scanner's OWN issue fingerprint is preferred
--   over one derived from title and URL. Burp documents it as stable across
--   scans; ours moves when an issue is renamed or reported on a different path.
-- 2025080493 (follow-up 687): rpc_scan_import closes a finding when the scanner
--   reports it DELETED. Burp's IssueEventType is found/deleted/updated — it
--   says when an issue is gone rather than merely stopping reporting it, so a
--   rescan can confirm a fix WITHOUT isolation. A deleted event is no longer
--   imported as a finding either.
-- 2025080492 (follow-up 686): a recurring finding keeps the scan_run_id of the
--   run that FIRST reported it. Reassigning it on every import moved findings
--   off earlier runs, so opening an older scan showed fewer findings than it
--   found and its counts shrank each time the target was rescanned.
-- 2025080491 (follow-up 679): 678 reverted. Burp does not re-report issues
--   already in its site map, so a smaller rescan is not evidence of fixes —
--   matching by host would have closed live findings as remediated.
-- 2025080489 (follow-up 676): scan_runs joins the realtime publication AFTER
--   the table exists. 611 listed it in the block near the top of the file,
--   which runs long before scan_runs is created, so the ADD failed silently and
--   nothing was ever published.
-- 2025080487 (follow-up 674): enable_checklists_runbooks defaults to true, and
--   existing tenants are switched on once. The curated templates ship with the
--   product, so a firm that never found the toggle never saw what it had.
-- 2025080486 (follow-up 673): the settings column-level SELECT grant is
--   recomputed at the END of the file. It only ever covered the columns that
--   existed where it ran, so every ADD COLUMN below it was ungranted and
--   PostgREST's `select=*` after an update was denied — saving settings broke
--   the moment any column was added.
-- 2025080485 (follow-up 665): the approved-target check aggregates DISTINCT
--   targets. The same address can be approved more than once (web application
--   AND external), so one requested target matched two rows and the count check
--   refused it — approving an asset twice made it unscannable.
-- 2025080484 (follow-up 656): the closing pass only closes a finding whose
--   ASSET this run examined again. A crawler does not cover the same ground
--   every time, so "not reported" was being read as "fixed" and a shallower
--   rescan marked live issues remediated.
-- 2025080483 (follow-up 655): client_scan_targets is GRANTed insert/update/
--   delete to authenticated — cst_write has always allowed staff, but the grant
--   was SELECT only, so Postgres refused every write before the policy was
--   consulted. Approving, editing and withdrawing all failed silently. A
--   customer may DELETE their own rows (withdrawing narrows scope); adding is
--   still refused.
-- 2025080482 (follow-up 654): scan_runs_read lets a client see a run THEY
--   requested, not only one marked client_visible. A scan they started was
--   invisible to them from creation while the cooldown — SECURITY DEFINER, so
--   no RLS — counted it: they were told to wait for a run they could not see.
-- 2025080481 (follow-up 652): scan_runs' foreign keys to profiles are REBUILT
--   with ON DELETE SET NULL. CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS skips an existing
--   table, so a tenant whose scan_runs predates that clause kept the original
--   constraint and could not delete anybody who had run a scan.
-- 2025080480 (follow-up 650): comment only — "Move into findings" is gone, so
--   the note explaining where a promoted finding got its code from described
--   something that no longer exists.
-- 2025080479 (follow-up 649): findings.client_id, backfilled and kept by a
--   trigger that DERIVES it from the assessment. Additive groundwork: a finding
--   can only reach a client through assessment_id today, which is why that
--   column is NOT NULL and why a scan invents a container assessment. Nothing
--   reads the new column yet.
-- 2025080477 (follow-up 647): rpc_kb_reapply — push an edited knowledge base
--   entry onto the SCAN findings it already covers. The reuse runs at import
--   time, so an edit reached nothing already imported and waiting for the next
--   scan is not an answer. Scan findings only: a tester's own finding may have
--   been edited by hand and overwriting that throws away their work.
-- 2025080476 (follow-up 646): rpc_scan_import reuses the knowledge base TITLE,
--   REFERENCES and CWE as well as the description and remediation, on first
--   import and on re-scan. The two earlier attempts failed because the TEST
--   HARNESS's kb_entries stub lacked those columns, not the schema.
